# .gitattributes -- line endings, and nothing else.
# Added at 2.20, 2026-08-28, by The Systems Engineer. Routed as item (a) of the Wave 2 open.
#
# THE DEFECT THIS CLOSES. Until today this repository had NO .gitattributes at any path. Every
# hook under tools/githooks/ begins `#!/usr/bin/env node`, and the only thing keeping that first
# line LF in the index was core.autocrlf=true happening to be set on the authoring machine.
# core.autocrlf is PER-INSTALL CONFIGURATION. .gitattributes is CONTENT. A clone whose autocrlf
# differs -- the default on Windows for many installs, and `input`/unset elsewhere -- checks the
# hook out with CRLF, and the kernel's shebang parser takes the carriage return as part of the
# interpreter path: `bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env node^M`. The hook does not run, git reports
# nothing useful, and every check it dispatches is silently unwired.
#
# THIS IS THE THIRD MEMBER OF ONE FAMILY, and naming the family is the point of writing it down.
#   E1        -- hooks are not cloned, so a hook in .git/hooks/ is a mechanism for one machine.
#   HK-2      -- a hook committed at 100644 is executable here and inert on a Linux clone,
#                because core.filemode is false on the authoring machine.
#   this file -- a hook committed with CRLF is executable here and inert on a Linux clone,
#                because core.autocrlf is true on the authoring machine.
# In all three the CONTENT is committed and the TRIGGER is metadata, and in all three the
# assertion that the mechanism exists passes on the machine where it cannot fail. The remedy is
# the same shape every time: put the property in the tree, not in the config.
#
# WHY THIS FILE IS NARROW. It governs the executable text this repository dispatches and nothing
# else. A blanket `* text=auto` would be a normalization pass over 108 tracked files during the
# merge wave, which is a diff nobody can review sitting on top of the diff that matters. The
# corpus is .md and .tsv and its line endings are not load-bearing; when someone shows that they
# are, that is a rule with a reason and it can be added then.

# --- the dispatchers and everything beside them -------------------------------------------
# eol=lf is stated in addition to `text` rather than relying on it: `text` alone normalises to LF
# in the INDEX and converts back on checkout according to the per-install setting, which is the
# exact dependence being removed. eol=lf pins the WORKING TREE.
tools/githooks/** text eol=lf

# --- everything this repository executes ---------------------------------------------------
# Same reason one level wider: a .js or .sh reached from a hook or a gate has the same shebang
# exposure the moment anyone marks it executable, and `sh script.sh` on CRLF fails in its own
# obscure ways ($'\r': command not found) long before anyone suspects line endings.
*.js text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf

# THE CORPUS. Added by the orchestrator at the Wave 2 close, 2026-08-28, and it is the same
# family as the three above. literature/ landed today with LF endings. core.autocrlf=true is set
# on this machine, so `git checkout` of a corpus file re-materializes it with CRLF -- observed,
# not theorized: restoring two files from HEAD converted them and MRG-4b immediately reported
# `no appended ## Provenance block` for both, because the provenance parser matches raw bytes.
# A FRESH CLONE WOULD DO THAT TO ALL 168. The corpus is content; its line endings are not
# per-install configuration.
literature/** text eol=lf
cr_scratch/merge_plan.tsv text eol=lf

# THE REGISTERS. Added at 2.18, 2026-08-28, by The Systems Engineer. FIFTH MEMBER OF THE FAMILY
# ABOVE, and it was measured rather than reasoned about, twice.
#
# MEASUREMENT 1, the dispatcher. tools/githooks/dispatch.js reads oracle/check_register.md with
# no CR normalisation -- `text.split('\n')` -- so the ninth and last field of every C row is the
# status cell with a carriage return stuck to it. Materialised the file with CRLF in a sandbox
# and parsed it exactly as the engine does: 38 rows parsed, `status === 'live'` matched ZERO of
# them and `status === 'specified'` matched ZERO of them. Field 9 of CHK-09 came back as the
# literal "specified\r". The engine obeys status by string equality, so with CRLF it skips
# nothing, dispatches every `specified` row, and calls fail() on the first artifact that does not
# exist. That is LOUD -- it blocks every commit on a fresh clone rather than passing quietly --
# and it is still a total stop of the pre-commit trigger on any install whose core.autocrlf
# differs from the authoring machine's. Loud is the better direction and it is not a defence.
#
# MEASUREMENT 2, and this one is not hypothetical at all: writing oracle/AMENDMENTS.tsv from a
# node script during this sub-step, git answered
#     warning: in the working copy of 'oracle/AMENDMENTS.tsv', LF will be replaced by CRLF the
#     next time Git touches it
# core.autocrlf=true is set here, so THIS repository will convert these files on the next
# checkout that touches them, exactly as it did to two corpus files at the Wave 2 close.
#
# WHY THIS SET AND NOT `* text=auto`. The narrowness argument at the top of this file still
# holds: a blanket rule is a normalisation pass nobody can review. These five paths are the ones
# a program parses FIELD-WISE, where a carriage return lands inside a value and is compared
# against a closed set. tools/check_registers.js already normalises (`.replace(/\r\n?/g,'\n')`)
# and is immune -- verified by running it against CRLF copies of all five, where AMC-1..AMC-5 and
# both size declarations passed unchanged. It is pinned anyway, because immunity that lives in
# one consumer is per-consumer configuration, which is the same mistake one layer up: the next
# parser written against these files will not know it has to strip \r, and CHK-26 and CHK-32 are
# both specified rows for parsers nobody has written yet.
oracle/check_register.md text eol=lf
oracle/AMENDMENTS.tsv text eol=lf
oracle/MANIFEST.tsv text eol=lf
oracle/VERIFIED.tsv text eol=lf
oracle/REGISTER.*.tsv text eol=lf
